ter","implicit":true,"key":"IbGjROh0wa"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-d39e47b56c4932adce4c3a98697199fb.mjs","model":{"question":"A real estate agent lists the prices of 15 homes sold last month in a neighborhood. Fourteen sold for between \\$350,000 and \\$500,000, and one mansion sold for \\$3.2 million. Which statement is correct?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The mean will be much larger than the median","feedback":"Correct! The mansion is a high outlier; it pulls the mean up substantially but barely affects the median."},{"correct":false,"text":"The median will be much larger than the mean","feedback":"It's the reverse—the median only depends on the order of the values, so the outlier hardly moves it."},{"correct":false,"text":"The mean and median will be approximately equal","feedback":"The two measures respond very differently to the outlier, so they will not be approximately equal."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Which measure factors in the actual magnitude of the mansion's price?","explanation":""},"key":"YnHQbn4PAL"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-d39e47b56c4932adce4c3a98697199fb.mjs","model":{"question":"A histogram of scores is clearly skewed left. What is the expected relationship between the mean and the median?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"Mean > Median","feedback":"In a skewed-left distribution the long tail is on the low side, pulling the mean down, not up."},{"correct":true,"text":"Mean < Median","feedback":"Correct! The long left tail pulls the mean below the median."},{"correct":false,"text":"Mean ≈ Median","feedback":"Mean and median are approximately equal only for symmetric distributions."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The mean gets pulled toward the long tail.","explanation":""},"key":"mYrj1HdMmD"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-d39e47b56c4932adce4c3a98697199fb.mjs","model":{"question":"For the ages of the Best Actress Oscar winners, the mean is 38.3 and the median is 34.5. What does this comparison suggest about the shape of the distribution?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The distribution is skewed right","feedback":"Correct! The mean is noticeably larger than the median, consistent with a right-skewed distribution (a few unusually old winners pull the mean up)."},{"correct":false,"text":"The distribution is skewed left","feedback":"If the distribution were skewed left, the mean would be below the median."},{"correct":false,"text":"The distribution is symmetric","feedback":"In a symmetric distribution the two measures would be approximately equal, but here they differ by almost 4 years."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Which direction is the mean pulled relative to the median?","explanation":""},"key":"SIoOkAsxaP"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-d39e47b56c4932adce4c3a98697199fb.mjs","model":{"question":"A journalist wants to report the \"typical\" household income in a city, where the income distribution is strongly skewed right. Which measure of center is more appropriate?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"The mean","feedback":"The mean is inflated by the few very high incomes and overstates what a typical household earns."},{"correct":true,"text":"The median","feedback":"Correct! The median is resistant to the high outliers and better represents a typical household."},{"correct":false,"text":"The mode","feedback":"The mode can be unstable for income data and ignores the center of the distribution."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Which measure resists the influence of a few extremely high incomes?","explanation":""},"key":"tWo5ITEPWS"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s Summarize","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"key":"vuA5Se4pHP"}],"identifier":"lets-summarize","label":"Let’s Summarize","html_id":"lets-summarize","implicit":true,"key":"gxTOPRZMUy"},{"type":"list"